# Drive through several poses in order (the patrol route), and say WHY when
# it cannot.
#
# Served by task_runner (syncai_task_runner, SyncAI-Robot-Workspace) on the
# relative name `navigate_through_goals` -> <robot_id>/navigate_through_goals,
# next to the UNCHANGED nav2_msgs/NavigateThroughPoses on
# <robot_id>/navigate_through_poses: same navigator code, same behavior tree
# (patrol.xml), same preemption rules; only the result and two feedback
# fields differ. NavigateToGoal is to NavigateToPose what this is to
# NavigateThroughPoses (2026-11): nav2's result is std_msgs/Empty, so an
# aborted PATROL step could tell the operator nothing, and the backend could
# not tell a waypoint in a keepout zone (permanent) from a person in the way
# (worth a retry). The backend migrates at its own pace; the nav2 action
# stays until it has.
#
# Field NAMES mirror nav2's NavigateThroughPoses on purpose (goal.poses,
# goal.behavior_tree, the six feedback fields): one navigator template in
# task_runner serves both actions, and it compiles only because the member
# names agree.

# The waypoints, in order, in the navigator's global_frame (map). Each pose's
# z picks its floor. The route passes through every waypoint without stopping
# or turning; only the last one is reached like a MOVE's goal (xy and its
# heading), the others' headings are ignored.
geometry_msgs/PoseStamped[] poses
# Path of a BT XML to tick instead of the default patrol.xml; empty = the
# default. Absolute, or a bare file name resolved against task_runner's own
# behavior_trees/ directory.
string behavior_tree
---
# Which stage failed. Meaningful ONLY with status ABORTED; SUCCEEDED and
# CANCELED carry NONE.
#
# ABORTED + NONE is not a navigation failure, exactly as for NavigateToGoal:
# superseded by a newer goal on this action, or rejected before the tree ran
# (the other navigators busy -- one navigation at a time across task_runner's
# actions -- an empty route, or a behavior_tree that could not be loaded).
#
# NavigateToGoal's layout and numbers, one table in four files (that one,
# this one, ComputePathToPose and ComputePathThroughPoses): keep them in step,
# never renumber one. ComputePathToPose documents each 2xx reason and whether
# a retry can help. On a route a waypoint's reason is the goal's
# (GOAL_IN_KEEPOUT: some waypoint lies in a zone) and error_msg names the leg.
uint16 NONE=0
uint16 UNKNOWN=1
uint16 FOLLOW_PATH_FAILED=100
uint16 PLAN_FAILED=200
uint16 INVALID_PLANNER=201
uint16 TF_ERROR=202
uint16 START_OUTSIDE_MAP=203
uint16 GOAL_OUTSIDE_MAP=204
uint16 START_OCCUPIED=205
uint16 GOAL_OCCUPIED=206
uint16 TIMEOUT=207
uint16 NO_VALID_PATH=208
uint16 NO_VIAPOINTS_GIVEN=209
uint16 MAP_NOT_LOADED=220
uint16 MAP_LOADING=221
uint16 START_IN_KEEPOUT=222
uint16 GOAL_IN_KEEPOUT=223
uint16 DISCONNECTED=224
# The docking range: never produced by patrol.xml, kept so the table is
# NavigateToGoal's whole.
uint16 DOCK_FAILED=300

uint16 error_code
# Human-readable, written for the operator console: a stage sentence with
# the BT node's detail in parentheses. Empty when error_code is NONE.
string error_msg
---
# nav2's six fields, same names, plus NavigateToGoal's two.
geometry_msgs/PoseStamped current_pose
builtin_interfaces/Duration navigation_time
builtin_interfaces/Duration estimated_time_remaining
int16 number_of_recoveries
float32 distance_remaining
# Waypoints not yet passed, the last one included until the route is done.
int16 number_of_poses_remaining
# Plans made for this goal beyond the first: the detours. Resets with every
# (pre-empted) goal.
uint16 number_of_replans
# What the navigator is doing right now: one English sentence written by the
# behavior tree. Free text: show it, never switch on it.
string status_msg
